{{also|Clinch}}
==English==

===Etymology===
16th-century alteration of {{m|en|clench}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/klɪntʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-clinch.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪntʃ}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[clasp]]; to [[interlock]]. {{defdate|from 1560s}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=9|passage=“Beloved shipmates, '''clinch''' the last verse of the first chapter of Jonah—‘And God had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.’”}}
# To make [[certain]]; to [[finalize]]. {{defdate|from 1716}}
#: {{ux|en|I already planned to buy the car, but the color was what really '''clinched''' it for me.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 29, 2011|author=Neil Johnston|title=Norwich 3 - 3 Blackburn| work=BBC Sport| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413656.stm| passage=Vincent Kompany was sent off after conceding a penalty that was converted by Stephen Hunt to give Wolves hope. But Adam Johnson's curling shot in stoppage time '''clinched''' the points.}}
# To [[fasten]] [[securely]] or [[permanently]].
#: {{syn|en|attach|join|put together|Thesaurus:join}}
# To [[bend]] and [[hammer]] the point of (a [[nail]]) so it cannot be removed. {{defdate|17th century}}
# To [[embrace]] passionately.
# To hold firmly; to [[clench]].
#: {{syn|en|clasp|grasp|grip|Thesaurus:grasp}}
# To set closely together; to close tightly.
#: {{ux|en|to '''clinch''' the teeth or the fist}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Duty of Servants at Inns|passage=try if the heads of the nails be fast, and whether they be well '''clinched'''}}

